<ManualLayout
  slug="terminals"
  title="The terminals"
  description="The operator console, tty01 to tty07, and why ipnx opens windows grouped by terminal shape rather than tabs."
>
  <h2>Nine ways in</h2>
  <p>
    The machine has an operator console and eight terminal lines, and ipnx lets you open all of
    them. This is not a metaphor: they are real lines on an emulated DZ11 serial multiplexer, each
    with its own <code>getty</code>, each independently loggable-in. Log in twice on different
    lines and you have two sessions on one machine, exactly as two people at two desks would have
    had.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Line</th><th>Terminal</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>console</code></td>
        <td>Operator console</td>
        <td>The boot transcript and kernel messages. Read-only.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tty00</code></td>
        <td>DMD 5620</td>
        <td>The bitmapped terminal. <code>TERM=dmd</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tty01</code>–<code>tty06</code></td>
        <td>vt100</td>
        <td>Ordinary glass terminals, 80×24.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tty07</code></td>
        <td>vt100w</td>
        <td>A wide one, 132 columns.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why the console is read-only</h2>
  <p>
    Because on this machine it genuinely is. Eighth Edition's <code>/etc/ttys</code> runs no
    <code>getty</code> on the console, so there is nothing there to type at — the boot messages
    arrive, and that is all it does. Presenting it as an input you could use would be a lie about
    the machine, so it is shown behind a lock.
  </p>

  <h2>Windows, not tabs</h2>
  <p>
    Terminals are grouped into windows by <em>shape</em>: the 5620 in one, the 80-column vt100s in
    another, the 132-column one in a third. Within a window they are tabs; across shapes they are
    separate windows.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is forced rather than chosen. Eighth Edition's kernel predates
    <code>TIOCGWINSZ</code> — the mechanism by which a modern terminal tells the system it has
    been resized — so nothing inside the machine can be told that a window changed size. A
    terminal is the size it was when it was created, permanently. Mixing an 80-column and a
    132-column session in one resizable window would mean one of them was always wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>A tab labelled tty03 really is tty03</h2>
  <p>
    Each line gets its own port on the emulated multiplexer, so the label tells you the truth. It
    matters more than it sounds: the login profile picks <code>TERM</code> from which line you are
    on, so if connections landed on arbitrary lines the machine's idea of what terminal you were
    using would depend on the order you opened tabs.
  </p>

  <h2>Closing one</h2>
  <p>
    Closing a window hangs up its lines, exactly as unplugging a terminal would. The session ends,
    the shell gets a hangup, and <code>getty</code> starts again on that line ready for the next
    login. Nothing else on the machine notices.
  </p>
  <p>
    Closing the 5620's window has a second effect worth knowing: it stops that emulated processor
    entirely and gives the CPU back. See <a href={href('/manual/the-5620')}>the DMD 5620</a>.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
